## 5. Advanced Concepts & Security

### Caching and TTL
To avoid asking the same question over and over, computers and resolvers "remember" (cache) DNS answers for a set amount of time, defined by the record's TTL (Time To Live). A short TTL means changes take effect quickly everywhere; a long TTL means better performance but slower updates when something changes.

### DNS Propagation
When you change a DNS record, it doesn't update everywhere instantly. It has to spread out across all the caching resolvers around the world, which can take anywhere from a few minutes to 48 hours, depending on TTL settings and how many servers have cached the old answer.

### Recursive vs. Authoritative Lookups
A **recursive resolver** does all the legwork of asking multiple servers to find an answer (steps 3-6 above). An **authoritative server** is the final source of truth for a specific domain â€” it doesn't guess or forward the question elsewhere; it simply gives the real, correct answer.

### DNSSEC (Domain Name System Security Extensions)
Normal DNS answers are not digitally signed, which means an attacker could potentially intercept a DNS request and return a fake, malicious answer (sending someone to a scam site that looks identical to the real one). DNSSEC adds a digital signature to DNS records, allowing resolvers to verify the answer is authentic and hasn't been tampered with along the way.

### DNS Spoofing / Cache Poisoning
An attack where a hacker tricks a DNS resolver into storing a fake, incorrect record in its cache. Anyone who then queries that resolver gets sent to the attacker's fake server instead of the real one â€” often used for phishing or intercepting traffic.

### DNS Hijacking
When an attacker gains control over a domain's DNS settings (often through a compromised registrar account) and redirects the domain to their own servers entirely, effectively stealing traffic or intercepting email for that domain.

### DDoS Attacks on DNS
Because so much of the internet depends on a small number of major DNS providers, attackers sometimes flood these providers with huge amounts of fake traffic (a DDoS attack) to overwhelm them, which can make many unrelated websites become unreachable all at once.

### DNS over HTTPS (DoH) and DNS over TLS (DoT)
Traditional DNS requests are sent in plain, unencrypted text, meaning anyone monitoring the network (like an ISP) can see which domains you're looking up. DoH and DoT encrypt these requests so that DNS lookups stay private.

### Split-Horizon DNS
A setup where a DNS server gives different answers depending on who's asking â€” for example, giving an internal, private IP address to employees inside a company's network, while giving a public IP address to everyone else on the internet.

### Reverse DNS (PTR Records)
While normal DNS turns a name into an IP address, reverse DNS does the opposite: it turns an IP address back into a domain name, useful for verifying server identity and reducing spam classification for email servers.

