## 7. DNS Servers & Roles

There isn't just one type of DNS server â€” different servers play different roles in answering a single lookup:

### Recursive Resolver (DNS Resolver)
The "middleman" server that receives your request and does all the work of tracking down the final answer, by asking other servers on your behalf. This is often run by your internet provider, or a public option like Google (`8.8.8.8`) or Cloudflare (`1.1.1.1`).

### Root Name Server
The very top of the DNS hierarchy. There are only 13 sets of root servers worldwide (though each is duplicated across many physical machines). Root servers don't know the final answer to anything â€” they simply point the resolver toward the correct TLD server.

### TLD Name Server (Top-Level Domain Server)
Handles one specific ending, like `.com`, `.org`, `.net`, or a country code like `.uk` or `.jp`. It doesn't know the exact IP address of every website either â€” it just knows which authoritative server is responsible for a given domain.

### Authoritative Name Server
The final stop. This server holds the actual, official DNS records for a specific domain and gives the real answer. When a company sets up their DNS, they are essentially telling their authoritative server what records to hold.

### Caching / Forwarding Server
Some smaller servers (like your home router or company network) don't do the full lookup process themselves â€” they simply forward requests to a bigger resolver and store (cache) the answers locally to speed up repeated requests.

